@node Condition DSL
@section Condition DSL

This DSL is present in JSON body checks and in SQL database checks, supported conditions include:

@code{non-empty-str} (ensures the field is not empty)

@code{empty-str} (ensures the field is empty)

@code{== x} (ensures the field is x)

@code{!= x} (ensures the field is not x)

@code{> 0} (ensures the field is greater than zero)

@code{>= 0} (ensures the field is non-negative)

@code{< 0} (ensures the field is smaller than zero)

@code{<= 0} (ensures the field is non-positive)

@node Row DSL
@section Row DSL

This is the DSL used to perform assertions on SQL databases. Currently PostgreSQL supports this.

@example
[0] ((non-empty-str)(>= 12)(== en_GB)(non-empty-str))
@end example

@menu
* Quantifiers::
* Conditions::
@end menu

@node Quantifiers
@subsection Quantifiers

@code{[0]} or @code{[first]} means the check should be applied to the first row in the result set

@code{[all]} means the check should be applied to all the rows in the result set

@code{[last]} means the check should be applied to the last row in the result set

@code{[n]} with n being a positive integer, means the check should be applied to the nth row in the result set

@node Conditions
@subsection Conditions

The rest of the clause is a tuple-based variant of the condition DSL, in order of selection in a query.

@node pop services
@section pop services

@code{pop_services} is a list of services to be started for the scenario.

A service is defined by:  

@code{id} (required, string) – A unique identifier for the service within the scenario. A common practice is to use a kebab-case version of the service name.  

@code{dir} (required, string) – The working directory (pwd) from which the service process should be started. This can be an absolute path or a path starting with @code{$HOME}, which will be expanded to an absolute path.  

@code{base_url} (required, string) – The URL where the service’s HTTP endpoint is reachable.  

@code{start_cmd_args} (required, list of strings) – The command(s) needed to start the service. These will be executed via a shell interpreter (default: @code{bash -c}). For example, if @code{make run} is specified, the system will actually execute @code{bash -c make run}.

@code{shell_path} (optional, string) - the path to the shell interpreter to use to run the service. This defaults to @code{bash}.  

@code{env_vars} (optional, map of string to string) – A set of environment variables to be defined for the service.  

@menu
* service health check::
* service postgres::
* service kafka::
* service keycloak::
* service pop@math{_server}::
@end menu

@node service health check
@subsection service health check

Defines how pop-test determines whether the service is healthy. Field is @code{health_check}, a required object.

Health checks can take different forms:

Dummy/Placebo health check:

@code{always_healthy} (boolean) – If set, forces the service to always be considered healthy (or never healthy).

HTTP health check:

@code{path} (required, string) – The HTTP route used to check the service’s health (e.g., @code{/health}).  

@code{patience_seconds} (required, integer) – The maximum time allowed for the service to become healthy. If the service is healthy before this deadline, the scenario setup continues without waiting.

@node service postgres
@subsection service postgres

Defines how PostgreSQL is used in the service. Field is @code{postgres}, an optional object.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to inject environment variables with PostgreSQL details

@code{host_var} (optional, string) - Container hostname will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_HOST}

@code{port_var} (optional, string) - Container DB port will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default ~POSTGRESQL@math{_PORT}

@code{db_var} (optional, string) - Container DB schema name will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_DB}

@code{user_var} (optional, string) - Container DB username will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_USER}

@code{pass_var} (optional, string) - Container DB password will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_PASSWORD}

@node service kafka
@subsection service kafka

Defines how Kafka is used in the service. Field is @code{kafka}, an optional object.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to inject environment variables with Kafka details

@code{bootstrap_server_var} (optional, string) - Container HTTP connection string for Kafka will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVER}

@node service keycloak
@subsection service keycloak

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to inject environment variables with Kafka details

@code{url_var} (optional, string) - Container HTTP connection string for Keycloak will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{KEYCLOAK_AUTH_SERVER_URL}

@code{realm_var} (optional, string) - Container realm name string for Keycloak will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{KEYCLOAK_REALM}

@node service pop@math{_server}
@subsection service pop@math{_server}

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to inject environment variables with Kafka details

@code{url_vars} (optional, list of strings) - List of environment variable names that will be injected to the service, with pop@math{_server}'s URL

@node pop actions
@section pop actions

@code{pop_actions} is a list of actions that setup state for a test and validate expected responses and behaviours from the services in the scenario. These actions help verify that the services return valid and expected data before proceeding with the scenario execution.

there are many types of pop action, see the following sections.

@menu
* a kafka produce action::
* an http assertion::
@end menu

@node a kafka produce action
@subsection a kafka produce action

A kafka produce action is defined by:

@code{message_file_path} (optional, string)

@code{kafka_topic} (required, string)

@node an http assertion
@subsection an http assertion

@code{expected_code} (optional, u16 number) - expected HTTP status code, defaults to 200

@code{assert_url} (required, string) – The endpoint path to be tested within the service. This should be a relative path, such as @code{/api/v1/forecast}, which will be appended to the base@math{_url} of the service.

@code{service_id} (required, string) – A reference to the id of the service being checked. This links the check to a specific service defined in @code{pop_services}.

@code{json_body_patterns} (optional, list of objects) – A list of conditions that must be met within the JSON response body. Each pattern defines a JSON field to inspect and a condition that must be satisfied.

Each JSON body pattern entry includes:

@code{pattern} (required, string) – A JSON path-like expression that identifies the field in the response body. This uses the Rust @code{jql} library.

@code{condition} (required, string) – A logical condition to be checked.

@node pop postgres
@section pop postgres

You can use PostgreSQL in pop-tests easily.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable PostgreSQL in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image name for PostgreSQL, by default @code{postgres}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for PostgreSQL, by default @code{17.2} or newer

@node pop kafka
@section pop kafka

You can use Kafka in pop-tests easily.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable Kafka in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for Kafka, by default @code{confluentinc/cp-kafka}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for Kafka, by default @code{6.1.1}

@node pop-server
@section pop-server

pop-server is a web-server that helps you simulate behaviours and test integrations with external systems.

pop-server is a lightweight HTTP server that allows dynamic behavior registration and retrieval. It acts as a simple puppet server where you can define request-response behaviors dynamically via HTTP requests. This can be useful for testing, mocking APIs, or creating programmable server responses.

Find the project here: @uref{https://codeberg.org/jjba23/pop-server}

You can use @code{pop_server} in pop-tests easily:

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable pop-server in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for pop-server, by default @code{jjba23/pop-server}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for pop-server, by default @code{0.3.0}


You can then  have it prepared with certain behaviors (under @code{behaviors} ):

@code{path} (required, string) - what URL path should be called to trigger behavior

@code{method} (required, string) - what HTTP method should be used to trigger behavior

@code{status} (required, string) - what HTTP status should be returned

@code{content_type} (required, string) - what content type should be returned as HTTP header

@code{body_file} (required, string) - path to the file to load data from, that will be returned as HTTP body

@node pop keycloak
@section pop keycloak

You can use Keycloak in pop-tests easily and have it auto-setup.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable Keycloak in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for Keycloak, by default @code{keycloak/keycloak}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for Keycloak, by default @code{26.1}

@code{realm} (required, string) – Name of the Keycloak realm to be created.

@code{client_id} (required, string) – OAuth ID of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{client_secret} (optional, string) – OAuth secret  of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{email}  (optional, string) – Email of the Keycloak user that will be created for service authentication.

@code{username} (required, string) - Username of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{password} (required, string) - Password  of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{roles} (optional, list of strings) - Names of roles to be created realm-wide and associated to the user

@code{scopes} (optional, list of strings) - Names of scopes to be created realm-wide and associated to the OAuth client

@node Why pop-test can be a Game-Changer
@chapter Why pop-test can be a Game-Changer

Software testing is an essential part of modern engineering, and pop-test takes it to the next level by making end-to-end testing simple, powerful, and reliable.

Whether you’re refactoring legacy code, maintaining a complex system, or ensuring business continuity, pop-test provides the confidence and efficiency your team needs.

@menu
* Engineering Freedom and Confidence::
* Reliable and Meaningful Test Coverage::
* Faster Development and Maintenance::
* Business Value and Risk Reduction::
@end menu

@node Engineering Freedom and Confidence
@section Engineering Freedom and Confidence

pop-test enables fearless refactoring.

Engineers can completely rewrite a system—even in a different way, or language, without second-guessing whether it still functions correctly.

With pop-test validating the expected behaviors, teams can iterate quickly and improve systems without introducing regressions.

@node Reliable and Meaningful Test Coverage
@section Reliable and Meaningful Test Coverage

Unlike mocks, which actually deliver few guarantees and are often more of a pain than a blessing, pop-tests verify the entire system as a whole.

By testing with real dependencies—databases, APIs, message queues, pop-test ensures that your application works as expected in real-world conditions.

This approach leads to fewer surprises in production and more stable software releases.

@node Faster Development and Maintenance
@section Faster Development and Maintenance

Maintaining a large codebase is much easier with pop-test in place. As systems evolve, tests remain valid, preventing accidental breakages and reducing the need for tedious manual verification.

Automating these processes saves valuable engineering time, allowing teams to focus on building new features instead of debugging regressions.

@node Business Value and Risk Reduction
@section Business Value and Risk Reduction

For businesses, software stability is critical. Bugs in production can lead to costly downtime, reputational damage, and loss of user trust.

pop-test mitigates these risks by catching issues early, before they impact customers.

With predictable and repeatable test automation, teams can ship faster with greater assurance, leading to a more agile and resilient business.

@node Conclusion
@chapter Conclusion

pop-test is designed for modern development teams who need robust testing without complexity. Its expressive DSL, seamless service orchestration, and extensibility make it a long-term solution for ensuring software quality across any technology stack.

With pop-test, you gain more than just a testing tool—you gain a strategic advantage in software development. Engineering teams become more productive, businesses gain confidence in their products, and maintaining large systems becomes easier than ever.

pop-test is not just about testing—it’s about enabling better engineering.

@bye
